## 9. Risks

- **Data scarcity / cold start**: a regional HVAC unit accumulates only tens of bids/year; public sources give competitor prices but not our costs. Mitigation: heuristic default, pooling across similar tenders, heavy regularization, wide bands, human decision preserved.
- **Scraping legality & ToS**: simap.ch is the official CH publication platform (simende.ch, cited in round 1, does not resolve at all — corrected); it is a JS single-page app, so collection needs headless rendering or XHR interception; rate-limit, cache, honor robots.txt (publication pages allowed); minimize personal data. TED/eForms and OCDS data are explicitly open; OpenTender's Swiss dump is CC BY-NC-SA (prototype only).
- **Selection bias / feedback loop**: we only observe outcomes for bids we submitted; optimizing expected contribution can ratchet margins down. Mitigation: clamps from params (`0.05–0.95`), randomization-free but monitored deployment, `_4` audits.
- **Concept drift** (market, inflation): recency-weight training; alert on calibration decay in `metrics.json`.
- **LLM numeracy & latency**: uncalibrated probabilities, slow inference on slider moves. Mitigation: calibrated tabular core, cached curves, browser interpolation (as designed above).
- **Golden-number pinning** *(resolved in round 4 — kept for history)*: rounds 2–3 believed the `expected_contribution_chf` arithmetic in `expected_output_example.json` was only reproducible within a tolerance (±1%, then ±0.5%); round-4 execution showed the golden is **exactly** reproducible — the residuals were artifacts of using the 2dp-rounded P (round 2) and of rounding the reference price to 100 CHF (round 3). `validate.py` pins the bid ceil-1000 rule, 2dp probabilities and contributions to the cent, with `R = baseline/(1−target_margin_pct)` unrounded (438612.83 for the golden subset).

